   "gap": "Durrett PTE author version 5 of 11 January 2019, p. 214 (PDF p. 222), between Theorems 4.1.16 and 4.1.17, states that regular conditional distributions do not always exist, attributes the first counterexample to Dieudonne (1948) with references to Doob (1953, p. 624) and Faden (1985), and explains the obstruction: countable additivity of P(X in A|G) holds only almost surely for each disjoint sequence, and when S has enough countable collections of disjoint sets the exceptional sets may pile up. The handbook existence theorem for spaces with a Borel real coding (regular-conditional-laws.tex lines 95-110) and its boundary (lines 250-257, which says only that the result is not an assertion for every measurable outcome space) state neither the non-existence fact, its historical attribution, nor the explanation, and none of these is supplied elsewhere on the route.",
