   "gap": "Durrett PTE author version 5 of 11 January 2019, Example 4.1.8 (p. 210, PDF p. 218), is Borel's paradox: for a point chosen at random on the earth it might at first seem that the longitude and the angle on the great circle should both be uniform; the longitude is uniform but the angle is not, and the paradox evaporates because the angle is independent of the longitude, so its conditional law is its unconditional, nonuniform law (more land near the equator than near the pole). The handbook theorem (regular-conditional-laws.tex lines 222-248, model lines 195-220) proves the coordinate laws and their independence but does not identify the example as Borel's paradox or state the paradoxical expectation that it resolves; the only route mention of the Borel paradox (bayes-factor-computation.tex) is unrelated to this example.",
